WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.162
Week twelve: measuring what matters.

00:00:03.212 --> 00:00:04.188
Nicky Barton,

00:00:04.238 --> 00:00:06.134
Head of Marketing Science at Mutinex,

00:00:06.184 --> 00:00:09.154
on how marketers work out what actually works.

00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:12.824
Mutinex helps brands find what drives sales.

00:00:12.874 --> 00:00:14.463
GrowthOS reads the market,

00:00:14.513 --> 00:00:15.984
Nicky's team translates,

00:00:16.034 --> 00:00:18.809
and brands decide where the next dollar goes.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.820
London, 1854.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.146
Cholera spread through Soho and experts blamed bad air.

00:00:25.196 --> 00:00:28.634
But data on where the deaths happened pointed to the water.

00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:31.217
Marketing had the same problem.

00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:33.198
For decades it ran on surveys,

00:00:33.248 --> 00:00:34.660
sales data months late,

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.651
and the gut feel of the most senior person in the room.

00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:39.885
Then tracking grew.

00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:41.454
Banner ads showed clicks,

00:00:41.504 --> 00:00:42.515
AdWords intent,

00:00:42.565 --> 00:00:43.529
analytics visits,

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:44.379
Facebook and the iPhone who you are,

00:00:44.122 --> 00:00:47.477
and the pixel what you buy.

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:52.138
So last touch became the default: the last click gets all the credit.

00:00:52.188 --> 00:00:55.933
It's a group assignment where whoever hits submit gets the whole mark.

00:00:56.633 --> 00:00:57.939
Picture this shopper.

00:00:57.989 --> 00:00:59.401
She saw a billboard, heard the radio, then clicked a search ad.

00:00:59.451 --> 00:01:05.791
The billboard gets zero credit, the search ad a hundred percent.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.773
So budgets flow to the bottom of the funnel,

00:01:08.823 --> 00:01:10.059
brand looks useless,

00:01:10.109 --> 00:01:11.934
and mental availability shrinks.

00:01:11.984 --> 00:01:16.570
Nike went direct, pulled back from retail, and people stopped seeing it.

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:19.139
Then privacy broke tracking.

00:01:19.189 --> 00:01:23.774
Safari limits, GDPR, then Apple's ask app not to track pop-up.

00:01:23.824 --> 00:01:28.009
Meta estimated that one pop-up cost it ten billion dollars in a year.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:29.500
Ask every platform how much it sold and the answers add up to over a hundred percent.

00:01:29.066 --> 00:01:35.410
Everyone did the assignment.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.925
Enter marketing mix modelling.

00:01:37.975 --> 00:01:44.635
Weekly sales, media spend, price, promotions, seasons, the economy and competitors go in.

00:01:44.685 --> 00:01:48.906
Out come what drove sales, ROI by channel and the next dollar.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.835
It's probabilistic, not deterministic.

00:01:50.885 --> 00:01:54.149
Not this click caused this sale,

00:01:54.199 --> 00:01:55.552
but the most likely effect,

00:01:55.602 --> 00:01:56.920
and how sure we are.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.742
More honest, and privacy safe.

00:02:00.433 --> 00:02:03.732
A cafe on a busy corner gets walk-ins with zero ads.

00:02:03.782 --> 00:02:05.088
That's baseline.

00:02:05.138 --> 00:02:07.246
And ads keep working after they stop,

00:02:07.296 --> 00:02:09.447
like a song stuck in your head.

00:02:10.133 --> 00:02:13.314
Add the fifth slice of pizza: returns diminish.

00:02:13.364 --> 00:02:16.333
Last year's star channel may not be the next best bet.

00:02:16.383 --> 00:02:19.555
And test first, like a flight simulator.

00:02:20.267 --> 00:02:24.910
Across eighty-three brands in Australia and New Zealand, many drifted to performance.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.328
Those keeping brand muscle grew most.

00:02:27.378 --> 00:02:33.121
Yet cinema, radio and free-to-air, the longest lasting channels, were cut first.

00:02:33.833 --> 00:02:36.519
If you can't add budget, don't add channels.

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:41.311
Spreading money thinner shrinks results, and brands that go off air pay later.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.188
Measurement has moved from the marketing report to the boardroom.

00:02:43.238 --> 00:02:46.820
The CEO asks what revenue and profit this created,

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:50.702
and the CFO trusts the budget.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.357
The numbers get easier to produce, so judgement becomes the job.

00:02:54.407 --> 00:03:00.966
The best marketers bring curiosity, comfort with numbers, commercial sense and storytelling.

00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:06.959
So: last touch misleads, privacy broke tracking, and modelling reads the whole market.

00:03:07.009 --> 00:03:10.839
Keep your brand muscle, and always ask, how do we know it worked?

00:03:10.889 --> 00:03:12.793
That's week twelve.
